    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d496">

                <pb facs="0690" n="610" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg610"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.801/674</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-03-18">March 18, 1936</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The French Ambassador handed me this memorandum<anchor n="6" xml:id="d496-f1"/>
                    today which refers to a bill recently introduced by Representative Bland, of
                    Virginia, the purpose of which is to prevent foreign vessels from touching at
                    American ports. This is a continuation of an effort which has already been made
                    in the past. The Ambassador pointed out, however, that if by any chance the bill
                    should become a law, it would invite retaliation on the part of foreign
                    governments with regard to American vessels touching, for instance,
                    Mediterranean ports. I think he had in mind in particular the winter cruises
                    which are becoming more and more popular every year in the Mediterranean.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
